1	# Game Example Standards
2	
3	Use this when building or reviewing Aura3D game examples.
4	
5	Read `llms.txt`, `docs/agents/claims-and-boundaries.md`, and
6	`docs/api/game-runtime.md` first. A route with a scene, keyboard listener, or
7	proof replay is not automatically a game.
8	
9	## Minimum Public Game Standard
10	
11	A public game example must prove:
12	
13	- player input visibly changes game state;
14	- reset works after normal play and after win/fail where applicable;
15	- there is a clear objective;
16	- there is scoring, timing, progress, or a fail condition;
17	- there is at least one real progression loop;
18	- primary character, vehicle, playfield, world, or track assets are typed
19	  GLB/glTF assets unless the game is explicitly abstract;
20	- route-health names renderer mode, fallback state, exact primary assets,
21	  primitive count, known limits, and claims;
22	- automated browser tests cover movement, restart, and at least one win, fail,
23	  score, lap, checkpoint, line-clear, collection, or completion mechanic;
24	- screenshots show first load, after input, mid-route, fail/reset when relevant,
25	  and finish/progression;
26	- the visual bar: the genre's look preset is applied (see
27	  `docs/agents/art-direction.md`), the frame shows environment light, a full
28	  key shadow, genre palette and fog/grade from the look — a mechanic-correct
29	  game on flat lighting and a void background is still `prototype`.
30	- all current release, visual-QA, and deploy

